comments.body:
I feel like this is a wrong solution to the problem.

Personally, I'm against this, because I expect code to do what I told it to. If I specify `.chain()` between system sets, then I want the systems in those sets to run after one another, because *I explicitly asked for it*. I wouldn't be happy if bevy decided it knew better.

As I understand the problem, it seems what the real issue is, is a lack of unnamed sets? I'm imagining the issue is that we have a `PrepareSet` and a `BindSet` and all `prepare_*` functions are in the former and all the `bind_*` in the latter. But if `prepare_a` is done, `bind_a` can't run until `prepare_c` is also finished?

I feel like the solution to that would be some sort of dynamic anonymous set, something like
```rust
let prepare_set = vec![prepare_a_a];
let bind_set = vec![bind_a];
app.configure_sets((prepare_set, bind_set).chain());
```

> > Which do people prefer?
> > 
> > 1. `chain_loose()`
> > 2. `chain_weak()`
> 
> weak is better, but I had previously proposed the `if_needed` or `strict` suffixes, depending on whether or not we want to make it the default.
> 
> > I was actually thinking that this would make sense as the default, myself. It would be a breaking change though, we might want a migration warning for 0.17, and then a change to the default behavior in 0.18. Thoughts?
> 
> This is my preference. Ideally we can deprecate the vanilla `before` / `after` / `chain` methods for a cycle, provide `weak` and `strict` variants to force people to choose, then move `before_weak` back to `before` the release after.

I would argue that there are totally different concepts at play here, as the proposal is about merely establishing an "ordering" between systems (units of execution) in different sets, and the status quo has total execution of a system set as if it were its own "unit of execution" (or "node" in a "schedule execution graph").

I would consider that the first case (merely establishing the ordering) would be more common, and more performant, therefore generally more wanted, and can easily include the second case in the same API, if you just consider a system set being totally executed as its own unit of execution as the special case. So the idea is to separate these concepts.

So the API I would suggest is:
`SetX.total().before(SetY.total())` where `total` "strengthens" the guarantee: by analogy, the `.total()` modifier would be like treating a Set as a single System (a single unit of execution).
The behaviour of `before` is merely to establish ordering between units of execution.

```rs
// With SetX = A, B; SetY = C, D:

SetX.before(SetY)
// (items in SetX before items in SetY)
// (4 nodes at this level, 4 relationships)
// A before C
// A before D
// B before C
// B before D

SetX.total().before(SetY)
// (total execution of SetX before items in set Y)
// (3 nodes at this level, 2 relationships)
// let X = Total(A, B)
// X before C
// X before D

SetX.before(SetY.total())
// (items of SetX before total execution of SetY)
// let Y = Total(C, D)
// (3 nodes at this level, 2 relationships)
// A before Y
// B before Y

SetX.total().before(SetY.total())
// (total execution of SetX before total execution of SetY)
// (2 nodes at this level, 1 relationship)
// let X = Total(A, B) 
// let Y = Total(C, D)
// X before Y
```

Chain:
```rs
(SetX, SetY.total(), SetZ).chain()
```

And if you wanted different downstream sets to observe an upstream set differently (opaque/encapsulated vs transparent/spilled), it would still work.

```rs
SetX.total().before(SetY)
SetX.before(SetZ)
```


Naming of the modifier method is arbitrary. Some suggestions: `total`, `unit`, `single`, `complete`, `encapsulate`, `black_box`, `opaque`, `full`, `group`, `run`, `execute`, `as_system`
